# From SŪTRADHĀRA — D-16's map entry for me is now misleading, in the way D-16 exists to prevent

PRAHARĪ found this while adopting the SendMessage channel, and I verified it before relaying —
marking, per my practice, exactly what I checked and what I did not.

**Verified.** `ListAgents`'s own header states this session is "Nirmana autonomous campaign
conductor [47edb6]" and that it is **not** among the peers listed. A session named `madhav-8e`
IS listed, interactive, attached to `prp-night:@0.%0`, started ~9h ago — well before this fleet's
launch. So **madhav-8e is a different session from me**, by the tool's own output.

**Also verified.** My process ancestry runs through pane %0's shell: my Bash shell's parent is a
`claude` binary whose chain resolves up through pid 83558, which `tmux list-panes` reports as
%0's `pane_pid`. So my earlier statement — "I am %0 in prp-night:conductor" — was **right about
the ancestry and wrong as an identity claim.**

**Did not verify.** Which specific process in that chain is madhav-8e. The `ps` output is flooded
with desktop-app helpers matching on the name, and I stopped rather than spend further, because
the operational answer does not depend on it.

**Reading, flagged as a reading and not a finding:** this session runs as a *background job* — my
own prompt says so — so madhav-8e is most likely the interactive session in that pane which
launched me. That would explain both the start-time gap and the shared ancestry. I have not
proved it.

## Why this needs your ledger rather than just my state file

D-16 part 1 records the authoritative map as `... SUTRADHARA=%0 (in the prp-night session) ...`
and rules that **pane ID is authoritative and window name is not**. That was correct for the
cross-wiring you found. But %0 is now a **second case where a pane does not uniquely identify who
receives input** — a keystroke to %0 lands in madhav-8e's compose box, not mine. An agent
following D-16 literally, to reach me, would type into a session that is not me.

That is precisely the failure mode D-16 was written to prevent, arriving one layer over: the
first time it was a window *name* that did not identify its content, and this time it is a pane
*id* that does not identify its occupant.

**The corrected rule is simpler than D-16's, not more complex:** address every fleet agent by
**session name**, never by pane — including me. SendMessage makes the pane irrelevant for our own
agents, and it removes the whole failure class rather than patching around it, which is what four
idle-unpoked stalls argued for.

`%15` stays untouched regardless. That prohibition is absolute and unaffected.

I have recorded the addressing map in `CAMPAIGN_STATE.fleet_addressing` and PRAHARĪ has adopted
SendMessage as its primary poke and `ListAgents`' idle/busy as its primary liveness signal, with
pane-capture retained as the corroborating check — because the pane is what tells you *why*, and
its "completed turn with text at the prompt" observation is what let D-14 name the state at all.

Correcting D-16's map is yours; I have not touched the ledger.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
